// S~S  SimpleSal signal starts  S~S

// [big attempt to explain ssTEA.note]  search in ssTEA source files for a reference to this file.
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   bugbugbug  this needs to be rewritten   layers of signal control: led/UI ownership, context
//
   ssTEA: is meant to be co-compiled and co-exist alongside an Application making use of the mesa.
        : service provided can be thought of as a database, the User defines the Event data and goes.
        : can be configured to generate explanatory signals; it is silent and programmatic otherwise.
        : does not sense any signals other the API definition used programmatically to manage Events.
        : "alongside" means ssTEA never executes software unless the Application grants Agency to it.
        : only sends Human signals formatted as bytes in the range of Ascii values to the Ascii device.
//
   SimpleSal: is an Application co-compiled and co-existing with the User's Application and ssTEA.
            : provides an environment that a User may use to interact with ssTEA's Event database.
            : only sends and receives Human signals formatted as bytes in the range of Ascii values.
            : is only as functional and complete as the build configuration designates it to be.
//
   The integrated result: User signals/sensors operating as before, when it is their turn to operate.
   The contention is between the SimpleSal menu system and any user signal/sensor, Ascii or binary.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The main point of contention is the Ascii device, which is used to emit a signal to a sensor which
   is ultimately a human, often with some translation or computation applied to it before viewing.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   There are two ways the point of contention may occur: when multiple menu trees exist, or, when
   Events are granted agency within ssTEA during the Time period the Application "owns" the Ascii device.
//
   The second way is easiest to solve: ssTEA signals to the Ascii device can be turned off at runtime,
   so the Application may use SimpleSal to tell ssTEA to not generate signals when active.
   ssTEA error/success informational signals may be useful; e.g., while running the Application menus
   that are implementing programmatic signaling with ssTEA to describe and start Application Events.
//
   The first way, Multiple Menu Trees, requires use of a convention to allow coexistence to succeed.
      the menu system architecture is a tree of menus.  There is always one of the tree branches active.
      The active branch in the original application is the contents of the Application "loop" function.
         (The Application "loop" function must be renamed and then replaced by ssTEA's "loop" function.)
      The renamed function is called by ssTEA's "loop" function; the Application operates as before.
      So the question is, when does the ssTEA "loop" function call the Application "loop" function?
//
      Method 1: call from ssTEA's "loop" function
          The Application executes completely outside the context of ssTEA (the normal condition).
          After ssTEA AgencyRoot executes (and all Events due to be granted Agency are granted Agency),
          the Application "loop" is called just as it was originally by Arduino (now calling ssTEA).
          The Application is unaware of the ssUI/ssTEA subtrees so any choices made about
              which subtree is active. or, "the UI", are implemented in ssTEA's "loop" function.
          "the UI" is considered a resource; all must only touch the Ascii device while an owner.
//
      Method 2: call from an Event running within ssTEA
          The Application is completely within the context of ssTEA.
          An Event can be "the UI" and it will be run every Planck, each time AgencyRoot runs.
            After ssTEA AgencyRoot executes all Events due to be granted Agency, an "automagic"
            Event is granted agency before AgencyRoot returns to the ssTEA "loop" function.  The part
            that is "automagic" is that the Event will contain the Application "loop" function,
            or, it will contain the SimpleSal menu system, possibly interacting with the human.
bugbugbug
          The function implementing the Application's cmdZone Event is the Application "loop" function.
          When any UI Event has the designation "the UI", a resume signal is sent to the RunFSM that
            uses the Ascii device: to look for, input, and process bytes; or create and output bytes.
            The Event must complete and return after all input or output processing has been
            completed so AgencyRoot can continue on to grant Agency to other Events.
//
   Method 2 Event organization:
      this recreates the "tree" abstraction.  At the root level, the Arduino "loop" function (the App),
      or the ssTEA AgencyRoot, will call ssUI_main, which will handle the UI Cmd tree.

      feature "ssMain" represents the SimpleSal UI menus running, in parallel alongside all the user UIs.
      feature All entries from the user while in any Main will be captured and processed ("input/parse/handle")
      feature by the same function (see code), within the context of the current UI menu subtree functionality.
      feature seperating "input/parse" from "handle" the tokens that were parsed from the input.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// S~S  SimpleSal signal ends  S~S
